UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 4, Lesson 1: Ancient societies: beliefs and daily life', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Ancient societies can be studied through beliefs, daily life and social structure, always limited by surviving evidence. Describe patterns from sources without claiming every person lived the same way. Avoid presenting religious ideas as facts about the supernatural; present them as beliefs recorded in sources.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nUsing class sources, name one belief practice, one daily-life activity and one social role, each with a source note.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content.\n\nWATCH FOR\nAssuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nBuild a three-column society chart (beliefs / daily life / structure) with six sourced entries and two ''unknown from these sources'' notes.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"History","learning_intention":"Ancient societies can be studied through beliefs, daily life and social structure, always limited by surviving evidence. Describe patterns from sources without claiming every person lived the same way. Avoid presenting religious ideas as facts about the supernatural; present them as beliefs recorded in sources.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HT4-2"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Build a three-column society chart (beliefs / daily life / structure) with six sourced entries and two ''unknown from these sources'' notes. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content. Reasoning: I used belief by attending to daily life rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised. Correction: Strong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content. Why: the correction restores accurate use of belief.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating belief on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with belief, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content. This matches the lesson skill for belief.","options":["Strong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content.","Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Using class sources, name one belief practice, one daily-life activity and one social role, each with a source note. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of daily life with clear evidence.","Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised.","Ancient societies can be studied through beliefs, daily life and social structure, always limited by surviving evidence. Describe patterns from sources…","Strong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising belief?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named daily life, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the daily life used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising belief. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Strong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about belief?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about belief.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about daily life?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: In a river valley society, irrigation shaped farming calendars; temple records may show elite religion more than private belief.…","Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Ancient societies can be studied through beliefs, daily life and social structure, always limited by… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised.","Build a three-column society chart (beliefs / daily life / structure) with six sourced entries and two ''unknown from these sources'' notes.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of belief?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating belief on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content. Reasoning: I used belief by attending to daily life rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with belief, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised. Correction: Strong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content. Why: the correction restores accurate use of belief.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-hsie-y7-w04-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise belief.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"History","success_criteria":["I can use belief with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify daily life and explain why it matters.","I can practise social structure independently on a new prompt.","I can notice evidence limit needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Ancient societies can be studied through beliefs, daily life and social structure, always limited by surviving evidence. Describe patterns from sources without claiming every person lived the same way. Avoid presenting religious ideas as facts about the supernatural; present…","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Ancient societies can be studied through beliefs, daily life and social structure, always limited by surviving evidence. Describe patterns from sources without claiming every person lived the same way. Avoid presenting religious ideas as facts about the supernatural; present them as beliefs recorded in sources.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about belief?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: In a river valley society, irrigation shaped farming calendars; temple records may show elite religion more than private belief. Daily life for labourers may appear mainly in art or archaeology.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUsing class sources, name one belief practice, one daily-life activity and one social role, each with a source note.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content.","explain":"Ancient societies can be studied through beliefs, daily life and social structure, always limited by surviving evidence. Describe patterns from sources without claiming every person lived the same way. Avoid presenting religious ideas as facts about the supernatural; present them as beliefs recorded in sources.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Ancient societies.","caption":"Evidence limits.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Belief</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">recorded idea</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Daily life</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">work food home</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example: In a river valley society, irrigation shaped farming calendars; temple records may show elite religion more tha Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example: In a river valley society, irrigation shaped farming calendars; temple records may show elite religion more tha Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about belief?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: In a river valley society, irrigation shaped farming calendars; temple records may show elite religion more than private belief. Daily life for labourers may appear mainly in art or archaeology. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Fair claims.","caption":"Not everyone identical.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Structure</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">roles/hierarchy</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Unknown</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">mark gaps</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about belief?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAssuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised. Here is a more productive model: Example: In a river valley society, irrigation shaped farming calendars; temple records may show elite religion more than private belief. Daily life for labourers may appear mainly in art or archaeology.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Three evidenced points; wording treats beliefs as beliefs; no invented sacred Australian content.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Assuming ancient art shows photography-like reality. Art can be symbolic or idealised. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about belief?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAncient societies can be studied through beliefs, daily life and social structure, always limited by surviving evidence. Describe patterns from sources without claiming every person lived the same way. Avoid presenting religious ideas as facts about the supernatural; present them as beliefs recorded in sources. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Transfer to a new inquiry"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 4, Lesson 1: Ancient societies: beliefs and daily life","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Investigating the ancient past","week":4,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: In a river valley society, irrigation shaped farming calendars; temple records may show elite religion more than private belief. Daily life for labourers may appear mainly in art or archaeology.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the sources, places or community details used.","wo